2026-11-01 13:22:16,137 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2511.18413: The paper addresses agentic recommender systems built on LLMs, which typically use either a single plan-and-execute agent or generic multi-agent task-decomposition roles, and therefore underuse collaborative filtering signals from user-item histories. The proposed Multi-Agent Collaborative Filtering (MACF) framework instantiates similar users and relevant items from the target user's history as LLM agents. Each agent has a profile, can call retrieval tools (GetSimilarUsers, GetRelevantItems, RetrieveByQuery, RetrieveByItem), suggests candidate items, and exchanges messages with other agents. A central orchestrator manages a multi-round discussion of at most five rounds. It dynamically recruits participating agents, issues personalized collaboration instructions, and runs a sufficiency test to decide whether to continue or end. On three domains (Clothing, Beauty, Music), MACF is reported to achieve the best hit rate
2026-11-01 13:22:16,138 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2605.25746: Existing LLM-based multi-agent systems either fix a coordination structure before execution, which limits adaptation as task states evolve, or adapt orchestration step by step while leaving coordination structure implicit and unstable as scale grows. Resource budgets, such as token limits, also shape the feasible coordination space but are often ignored. The paper proposes MACA, which treats coordination as posterior inference over structure and orchestration. Its Structural Prior Learning component, GraphSpec, learns a task- and budget-conditioned prior that estimates agent relevance and interaction plausibility, producing a probabilistic interaction graph that constrains agent participation and information flow. Its Token-Aware Orchestration component then selects agents step by step within this reduced space, with the structural prior acting as a constraint through reward modulation. Across six benchmarks, MACA outperforms adaptive multi-agent baselines by an average of 8.42% while using 43.19% fewer tokens. The authors report that joint adaptation of structure and orchestration suppresses redundant interactions. The stated limitations are that evaluation centers on relatively structured benchmarks, so generalization to open-ended or domain-specific settings is not established. MACA also relies on a predefined agent pool with manually specified capabilities, so some gains may come from agent initialization rather than coordination alone. Finally, GraphSpec is trained on filtered high-quality trajectories and final-outcome signals, which may favor easily verifiable patterns and reduce robustness where credit assignment is noisy or intermediate collaboration matters.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,138 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2308.00352: MetaGPT addresses the problem that LLM-based multi-agent systems handle simple dialogue tasks but struggle with complex ones, where naively chained LLMs produce logic inconsistencies from cascading hallucinations and unproductive chatter. Its approach encodes human Standardized Operating Procedures (SOPs) into prompt sequences and models a simulated software company. Agents with roles such as Product Manager, Architect, and Engineer work in an assembly-line paradigm, producing structured intermediate outputs (e.g., requirements documents, design artifacts, flowcharts, interface specifications) that allow verification at each step. Agents share information through a message pool and subscriptions, and a runtime executable feedback mechanism runs and debugs generated code. The excerpt does not detail the communication topology beyond these mechanisms.

On HumanEval and MBPP, MetaGPT reports Pass@1 scores of 85.9% and 87.7%, which the authors describe as state of the art. Executable feedback yields a 5.4% absolute improvement on MBPP. The authors also report a 100% task completion rate and claim advantages over AutoGPT, LangChain, AgentVerse, and ChatDev on higher-complexity software projects, though the excerpt gives no numbers for those comparisons.

The excerpt does not explicitly state limitations
2026-11-01 13:22:16,139 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2406.08979: The paper addresses a limitation of single-team LLM agent systems. In phased pipelines such as software development, a team follows one predefined configuration and produces a single decision path, which can repeat errors and limit exploration of the solution space. The authors also argue that graph-based self-organizing approaches require extensive task-specific customization.

The proposed Cross-Team Orchestration (Croto) runs multiple teams on the same task. Within each team, instructor and assistant agents hold chained multi-turn dialogues across sequential phases. Teams first propose solutions independently, then exchange insights at selected phases. A Hierarchy Partitioning mechanism groups the teams' solutions, and a Greedy Aggregation mechanism synthesizes them into a final outcome. A pruning mechanism is also used. The framework is evaluated on 15 software-generation tasks from the SRDD dataset and 10 story-generation tasks from ROCStories.

The authors report significant improvements in software quality over state-of-the-art baselines and in story quality. The provided text, however, gives no numerical scores, effect sizes, or baseline values, so specific gains cannot be stated from it. The authors credit team diversity and cross-team collaboration for the gains.

The excerpt contains no explicit limitations section for Croto. The only constraints it discusses are those of prior single-team and graph-based approaches.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,141 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2505.19591: The paper addresses the rigidity of static or predefined agent topologies in LLM-based multi-agent systems, which the authors argue cause coordination overhead, redundant computation, and weaker performance as task variety and agent count grow. Its approach is a "puppeteer-style" paradigm in which a centralized, learnable orchestrator routes "puppet" agents one step at a time based on the evolving task context. Routing is framed as a sequential decision problem that yields an implicit inference graph, described as a directed graph-of-thoughts. The orchestrator's policy is updated with reinforcement learning using feedback from completed tasks, so it learns to prioritize effective agents and suppress less useful ones. The authors report that experiments on closed- and open-domain scenarios show superior performance at reduced computational cost, and that gains are associated with more compact, cyclic reasoning structures. The provided excerpts do not give specific numerical results, the individual agent roles, or any explicit limitations section.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,142 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2410.21784: MARCO addresses the difficulty of using LLMs for complex, multi-step task execution, where outputs are non-deterministic and prone to inconsistent formatting, function and parameter hallucination, and missing domain knowledge. Its coordination approach, as described in the excerpt, uses symbolic plans written in natural language as task execution procedures (TEPs) to guide agents through conversations. Tool execution follows deterministic graphs that provide status updates and request clarification from the user. A shared hybrid long-term memory stores context, TEPs, tool updates, and conversation turns across agents. Guardrails validate tool invocations and use reflection to recover from common LLM errors. The excerpt does not define specific agent roles or a detailed communication topology. On the Digital Restaurant Service Platform (DRSP) and Retail conversation datasets, MARCO reports 94.48% and 92.74% task execution accuracy. Relative to a single-agent baseline, it reports accuracy gains of up to 11.77% (DRSP-Conv) and 4.36% (Retail-Conv), 44.91% lower latency, and 33.71% cost reduction. The conclusion also cites a +30% accuracy improvement from reflection guardrails, without specifying its baseline. The excerpt contains no explicit limitations section. It claims the design is modular and generic, but evaluation is reported only on these two datasets.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,143 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2501.06322: The paper addresses the limitations of individual LLMs, including hallucination, autoregressive generation that hinders slow reasoning, and scaling-law constraints, and proposes multi-agent systems (MASs) of LLM-based agents as a route to collective intelligence. It surveys collaboration in these systems and proposes an extensible framework that characterizes collaboration along five dimensions: actors, types (cooperation, competition, coopetition), structures (peer-to-peer, centralized, distributed), strategies (e.g., role-based or model-based), and coordination protocols. The provided excerpt does not detail specific agent roles, communication topologies, or control-flow procedures for particular systems. The abstract says the review covers applications in 5G/6G networks, Industry 5.0, question answering, and social and cultural settings. The excerpt reports no quantitative results. Claimed benefits, including knowledge distribution, long-term planning, generalization through specialized personas, and interaction efficiency, are stated qualitatively. The paper names open challenges, lessons learned, and research directions, but the excerpt does not enumerate them, so no explicit limitations of the survey itself are stated.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,144 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2507.08616: AgentsNet addresses the lack of benchmarks that test whether LLM multi-agent systems can coordinate and collaborate over a network topology, beyond measuring accuracy on standard reasoning tasks. It is built from five distributed-computing problems: graph coloring, minimal vertex cover, maximal matching, leader election, and consensus. Agents occupy nodes in a graph and exchange messages with their neighbors over multiple rounds. The evaluated baselines include homogeneous agent networks that must first agree on organization and communication protocols. Graph instances are sampled from small-world and preferential-attachment models. A three-agent example (Emily, Zach, and Tom) shows agents sending conflicting leadership claims, illustrating the need for agreement. Some frontier LLMs perform strongly on networks of 4, 8, and 16 nodes but decline as network size grows, and the authors probe models with up to 100 agents. Figure 1 plots mean score against API cost per repeat and marks Pareto-optimal models, but the excerpt gives no exact score values. The authors state that the tasks are challenging even for the best models. The excerpt contains no dedicated limit
2026-11-01 13:22:16,144 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2508.00032: This paper asks whether explicit communication between LLM-based agents amplifies or mitigates language-driven biases in strategic cooperation. Using an extended FAIRGAME framework, the authors compare interactive and non-interactive conditions in one-shot and repeated games. Agents carry distinct strategic, linguistic, and personality traits. The study uses the Prisoner's Dilemma and the Battle of the Sexes, with GPT-4o and Llama 4 Maverick across three languages. The excerpts do not specify the communication topology or detailed control flow beyond this comparison. Communication substantially changes behavior: it fosters cooperation and improves outcomes in the Prisoner's D
2026-11-01 13:22:16,145 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2406.20041: The paper addresses the difficulty of deploying LLM agents in enterprise settings. LLMs lack access to confidential business data, fine-tuning on company data raises preparation and maintainability challenges, and production use requires reliability across mixed modern and legacy IT systems. The authors propose BMW Agents, a modular framework built on ConvPlanReAct, an extension of ReAct with dialog capability. A Planner Agent decomposes a user request into a task queue. A Semantic Matcher selects the agent best suited to each task, and a Mention Matcher handles explicit message passing. In the software development example, three agents (Coder, Architect, Tester) each hold a distinct tool (File I/O, Web Search, Code Execution) and can converse with one another, and a Verifier Agent checks the final output against the original request. Other mechanisms include tool-list refinement, episodic memory retrieval, human feedback integration, and resumption of paused workflows from the last executed task.

The provided text reports no quantitative results or benchmark numbers. The software development example is presented as an illustrative workflow, not an evaluated one.

The excerpt contains no explicit limitations section. The authors state only that the framework aims to close gaps in existing agent frameworks that may hinder production-scale applications, and they describe the report as a technical introduction and blueprint.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,146 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2502.14743: This survey addresses the lack of a unified account of multi-agent coordination across application domains. Prior reviews mostly classify algorithms by technique or task, rarely by the shared questions of who to coordinate with and how to coordinate. The authors propose a single computational framework built on four questions (what, why, who, and how). In this framework, clustering answers "who," and managing agent dependencies answers "how." The survey reviews three general coordination problems and six applications, including search and rescue, warehouse automation, transportation, humanoid robots, satellite systems, and LLMs. The excerpt does not describe agent roles, communication topologies, or control flows for any specific system, and it reports no benchmark results or performance figures. Its only quantitative content is a Web of Science count showing MAS research across 148 of 252 research areas. The authors identify scalability, heterogeneity, and learning mechanisms as open challenges, and propose hybridizing hierarchical and decentralized coordination, human-MAS coordination, and LLM-based MAS as future directions. The excerpt does not explicitly state the survey's own limitations.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,147 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2604.00249: Single-agent LLM systems struggle to support the diverse conversational functions of behavioral health communication while maintaining safety, and existing multi-agent work often emphasizes task performance or reasoning chains over dynamic, multi-turn dialogue. The proposed framework decomposes supportive dialogue across specialized agents, including empathy-focused, action-oriented, and supervisory roles. A prompt-based controller dynamically activates the relevant agents and enforces continuous safety auditing at each interaction. The paper does not specify the communication topology beyond this controller-driven activation and supervision. The authors evaluate the framework on semi-structured interview transcripts from the DAIC-WOZ corpus, using scalable proxy metrics for structural quality, functional diversity, and computational characteristics, compared against a single-agent baseline. The reported results are qualitative: clear role differentiation, coherent inter-agent coordination, and predictable trade-offs among modular orchestration, safety oversight, and response latency. The excerpts provide no numerical values. The authors state that the work is a simulation and analysis tool rather than a clinical intervention or therapeutic evaluation. Evaluation relies on proxy metrics, not validated clinical outcomes. The authors also identify future work on adaptive orchestration, richer inter-agent interaction patterns, and human-in-the-loop evaluation.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,148 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2310.08067: GameGPT targets two LLM failure modes in automated game development: hallucination, which prior work has flagged in planning and code generation, and redundancy, which the authors identify as an additional problem, where models produce unnecessary tasks or code. The authors argue that a single LLM covering the whole development cycle would be impractical and costly, and that long script generation increases both failure modes.

The framework is a multi-agent pipeline with distinct roles: planning and task identification, a game engine engineer that generates code, a code reviewer that checks for deviations and hallucinations and suggests revisions, and a testing engineer that executes tasks in sequence, aggregates logs, and reports tracebacks. Users give feedback through a frontend, which the reviewer incorporates iteratively. "Dual collaboration" pairs LLMs with small expert deep learning models and execution roles with review roles. Code is decoupled into short Lua snippets, with K candidates per task tested in a virtual environment and filtered by test results and user feedback. An in-house, annotated snippet lexicon supports instruction tuning.

The excerpt reports no quantitative results; it only states that empirical results demonstrate effectiveness in decision-making and rectification. It also contains no explicit limitations section, so none are reported here.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,149 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2606.13598: Training LLM-based orchestrators for multi-agent systems (MAS) is hard because human annotations of orchestration quality are scarce and full sub-agent rollouts are costly. For example, the MAS-Orchestra GRPO training consumes over 1B tokens for 100 orchestrator update steps. The proposed Orchestration Reward Modeling (OrchRM) is a self-supervised framework that operates at the orchestration level. An LLM-based controller dynamically plans the workflow and agent interactions, but the excerpt does not specify agent roles, communication topology, or control flow in detail. OrchRM builds win-lose pairs from intermediate artifacts (model checkpoints and trajectories) of MAS-Orchestra training and trains a Bradley-Terry reward model. It is applied to best-of-N selection at inference and to reward-guided orchestrator training. Reported results include up to 10x improvement in training token usage and up to 8% higher MAS test-time accuracy, with gains across mathematical reasoning, web-based QA, and multi-hop reasoning. Reward-guided continued training improves MAS-Orchestra, and training from scratch with reward-model feedback approaches the teacher model's performance. Limitations: performance is constrained by the diversity and quality of available orchestrator models and sampled trajectories, and OrchRM is trained in a domain-specific manner, with multi-domain training left underexplored.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,149 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2509.11079: The paper addresses a limitation of existing LLM multi-agent frameworks: static or task-level workflows either over-process simple queries, wasting resources, or underperform on complex ones, and they ignore cost-performance trade-offs across heterogeneous LLMs. The proposed Difficulty-Aware Agentic Orchestration (DAAO) generates query-specific workflows guided by predicted query difficulty. Its three modules are a variational autoencoder that estimates difficulty, a modular operator allocator, and a cost- and performance-aware LLM router that assigns different models to operators. A controller network samples architectures from a continuous "agentic net" conditioned on the query. A self-adjusting policy lowers predicted difficulty after a successful workflow and raises it after a failure, without manual labels. The excerpts do not specify the agent role definitions or the inter-agent communication topology. Across six benchmarks (HumanEval, MBPP, GSM8K, MATH, MMLU, GAIA), DAAO surpasses automated orchestration methods by 3.5% to 15.2% and LLM routing methods by 3.2% to 10.2%. On MATH, it outperforms MasRouter using 65% of its training cost and 41% of its inference cost. The conclusion reports up to 11.21% higher accuracy with up to 36% lower cost. The provided text has no dedicated limitations section; the only related item is future work on extending DAAO to multi-modal queries and incorporating real-time feedback.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,150 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2509.05882: This paper examines how LLM alignment methods affect an AI agent's reliability as a partner in multi-turn, multi-party collaboration. The authors argue that common alignment techniques, developed for single-user settings, lack optimality guarantees under a modified-action MDP, which models how collaborator modifications shift the action space. The approach uses roleplay simulation in which several LLMs act as collaborators solving tasks through human-readable dialogue. Differently aligned intervention agents insert themselves into the group conversation, not to give answers but to add "friction" that prompts participants to slow down, reflect, and clarify their assumptions. The excerpt does not specify the control flow, turn-taking policy, or communication topology beyond this insertion. The authors report that the FAAF method, designed for friction interventions, outperforms other alignment baselines in balancing common-ground convergence with correct task solutions, and that it is robust to collaborator action modification. The excerpt gives no numerical scores. The study is limited to AI-AI interactions in simulation on two collaborative tasks. Real human subjects, larger or more open-ended hypothesis spaces, and possible exposure-bias effects are left to future work.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,150 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2605.13110: The paper addresses venture capital due diligence, where analysts must synthesize fragmented sources, including web data, news, and official registry filings, through manual desk research that is slow and error-prone. The authors propose an event-driven multi-agent framework in which a single analyst trigger starts a pipeline that decomposes due diligence into specialized sub-tasks handled by distinct AI agents, with real-time search APIs supplying market intelligence. A custom module reverse-engineers the frontend-to-backend endpoints of the Greek Business Registry (Γ.E.MH.) to retrieve PDF filings, which a layout-aware OCR extractor parses with preserved source citations. The pipeline ends with an HTML report delivered by email, and a structural fallback flags missing data rather than generating unverified figures. The excerpt reports no quantitative evaluation results and does not detail individual agent roles or the communication topology. The conclusion's proposed extensions imply several limitations: the system is not yet integrated with the fund's internal portfolio database, its registry coverage is limited to Greek companies, and it depends on a third-party PDF extraction service that the authors plan to replace with a self-hosted open-source alternative.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,151 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2608.08605: The paper addresses the lack of a common process-level basis for comparing LLM-based multi-agent systems (MAS). Outcome-only benchmarks such as GSM8K, MMLU, and HumanEval reduce a run to one final answer, while LLM-as-Judge evaluation is model-dependent, costly, and hard to reproduce. The proposed framework maps each MAS trace to a directed acyclic collaboration graph, where nodes are agent actions and edges encode communicative or information-flow dependencies. Candidate graphs are then compared with a query-specific reference forest of verified-success graphs. The instantiated benchmark, ForestBench, filters 844 collaboration-necessary queries from seven public datasets and precomputes ten successful reference graphs per query, drawn from six representative MAS paradigms (including debate, planner–specialist pipelines, and peer swarms) plus a single-agent anchor. Six MAS frameworks are evaluated with
2026-11-01 13:22:16,151 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2511.20639: The paper asks whether LLM-based multi-agent systems (MAS) can collaborate entirely in continuous latent space rather than through natural-language text. It proposes LatentMAS, a training-free framework in which each agent generates auto-regressive latent thoughts from last-layer hidden embeddings instead of decoded tokens. Agents share a latent working memory, stored as layer-wise KV caches, that carries each agent's input context and latent thoughts to others without re-encoding. The framework is evaluated in sequential and hierarchical MAS settings, but the excerpt does not specify individual agent roles or detailed control flow. Across 9 benchmarks covering math and science reasoning, commonsense understanding, and code generation, and five Qwen3 and Llama3 backbones, the authors report accuracy gains of up to 14.6%, output token reductions of 70.8% to 83.7%, and 4× to 4.3× faster end-to-end inference relative to single-agent and text-based MAS baselines. The excerpt does not include an explicit limitations statement, so none can be reported from it.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,151 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2405.14314: The paper addresses how to ground LLM planners for embodied multi-agent collaboration. Existing closed-loop methods rely on self-reflection or physical verification, which require excessive LLM queries and environment interactions. The proposed Reinforced Advantage (ReAd) framework trains a critic network by regression on LLM-planned data to estimate advantage values, then prompts the LLM planner to act as an optimizer that generates plans maximizing that advantage. Agents such as Alice and Bob negotiate through multi-agent dialog, and the critic scores each proposed plan. If the advantage falls below a threshold, the LLM re-plans. ReAd has two variants: ReAd-J evaluates joint plans for all agents at once, while ReAd-S has agents generate actions sequentially, using multi-agent advantage decomposition. On Overcooked-AI and a harder variant of RoCoBench (DV-RoCoBench), the authors report that ReAd surpasses baselines in success rate and significantly reduces agent interaction steps and LLM query rounds. The excerpt gives no specific numerical values. Stated limitations include experiments limited to 2- or 3-agent scenarios, the expected exponential growth of ReAd-J's joint state-action space with more agents, and ReAd-S's higher computational and data-collection costs. Scalability to many-agent settings is left to future work, which also includes multi-objective and safe planning.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,152 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2508.15876: DeepMEL addresses multimodal entity linking (MEL), which links textual and visual mentions to entities in a multimodal knowledge graph. The authors cite three challenges: fragmented long-range textual context, coarse fusion-then-matching of text and image features, and the difficulty of coordinating LLMs, which excel at text, with LVMs, which excel at visual perception. DeepMEL uses four role-specialized agents. Modal-Fuser produces a context summary with an LLM and a structured image description through LVM visual question answering. Candidate-Adapter filters candidates with embedding-retrieval tools and iteratively refines the Top-K set to balance recall and precision. Entity-Clozer casts linking as a structured cloze prompt for the LLM to select the entity. Role-Orchestrator is described as the central scheduling module that coordinates task distribution and model updates among agents. The text does not specify the communication topology in further detail. On five public benchmarks, DeepMEL reportedly achieves state-of-the-art results, improving ACC by 1% to 57%, and ablations are said to verify each module. The provided text contains no explicit limitations section, so none are reported here.
2026-11-01 13:22:16,152 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2505.11765: The paper addresses the reliance on handcrafted design in LLM-based multi-agent systems (MAS), where agents and collaboration structures are typically fixed by human expertise or empirical findings. Prior work optimizes single-agent prompts or fine-tuning, or searches over structures without supervised signals (DyLAN) or only architectures (G-Designer, MaAS). The proposed OMAC framework defines five optimization dimensions: two for agent functionality (optimizing existing agents and constructing new ones) and three for structure, managed by LLM-based controllers that select candidate agent teams, choose agents for each collaboration step, and govern inter-agent communication. Two LLM actors drive optimization. A Semantic Initializer generates an initial pool of agents or controllers, and a Contrastive Comparator reasons over high- and low-scoring pairs from training-set collaboration runs to produce refined candidates, iterating this cycle. A separate algorithm performs joint optimization across dimensions. The excerpt reports that OMAC outperforms strong baselines on general reasoning, code generation, and arithmetic reasoning, and that joint optimization further improves performance, but it gives no specific numerical scores. The excerpt contains no dedicated limitations section. The authors note that "optimize" is used colloquially to mean improvement rather than guaranteed optimality.
