2026-11-01 13:22:16,153 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2404.01663: The paper addresses the dependence of LLMs on carefully written human prompts, which the authors show can sharply degrade performance, along with high compute demands, data biases, and limited robustness that hinder deployment in resource-constrained settings. It proposes the Collaborative Multi-Agent Tuning (CMAT) framework, in which agents with specialized roles are assigned tasks through a role-playing mechanism, share insights, and adapt through weight updates driven by environmental feedback, with dynamic memory updates. The TinyAgent models, fine-tuned from Qwen and Codellama on a curated dataset, are the trained agents. The excerpt does not specify the communication topology or control flow. TinyAgent-7B performs on par with GPT-3.5 despite having fewer parameters and rivals GPT-4 and AgentLM in certain scenarios. An ablation across six agent tasks (OS, DB, KG, ALF, WS, M2W) shows full TinyAgent-7B scoring 27.3, 43.0, 38.0, 10.0, 61.8, and 14.0. Agent-only tuning drops to 20.1, 39.3, 25.0, 2.0, 55.7, and 7.0, and general-only tuning falls to 9.7, 5.4, 0.0, 0.0, and 26.6, with no M2W value reported
2026-11-01 13:22:16,153 - INFO - > Generated summary for doc 2505.12467: This paper addresses a gap in LLM-based multi-agent systems: existing frameworks emphasize architectural structure and role assignment, while the granular mechanics of how agents interact, share context, and reach consensus remain underexplored. The authors formalize four collaboration dimensions: governance (centralized, with an instructor agent coordinating, versus decentralized self-organization), participation in discussion rounds (full versus selective), interaction patterns (broadcast, targeted peer messaging, or ordered turns), and context management (full dialogue history versus summarized history). They evaluate these strategies in two scenarios, Distributed Evidence Integration (DEI) and Structured Evidence Synthesis (SES), measuring task accuracy and computational cost, and introduce a Token-Accuracy Ratio (TAR) to quantify trade-offs. The authors conclude that centralized governance, instructor-led participation, ordered interaction, and instructor-curated summarization best balance accuracy and efficiency. Centralized governance with instructor-led participation and context management reduces token costs by up to 93.0% while maintaining accuracy the authors describe as better, though the provided text gives no accuracy figures. Decentralized systems show higher variance and inefficiency, particularly in SES, where misaligned agents degrade performance. Stated limitations include dependence on the quality and completeness of contextual data, coordination and efficiency challenges as agent count grows, the assumption that agents interpret context independently, uncertain generalization beyond the specific tasks, no integration of external knowledge, and difficulty tracing the rationale behind agent interactions.
